Teach the vendored gyp to compile Windows with clang-cl, and to know arm64

Node 24 and newer require clang-cl on Windows (BUILDING.md: "As of Node.js 24.0.0,
ClangCL is required to compile on Windows"), but `./configure --clang-cl=<version>`
only records the toolchain in the generated config - it does not change which
compiler ninja runs. gyp's ninja generator looks cl.exe up through the Visual Studio
environment and keeps using it, while the flags Node asks for are chosen by the
`clang=1` variable that same option sets. The result is clang-style flags compiled by
MSVC, and no C++ standard at all, because Node expresses the standard in
`msvs_settings`' LanguageStandard, which gyp marks MSBuild-only and the ninja
generator drops.

gyp's only knob for another compiler on Windows is a `make_global_settings` CC entry
whose path ends in "clang-cl", and it cannot be used from Node: gyp requires that
entry to be identical in the top level of every .gyp file of the build
(generator/ninja.py asserts "make_global_settings needs to be the same for all
targets"), Node's build files share no single top-level include, and node.gyp alone
cannot carry it. The path is therefore taken in here instead, from
`-G clang_cl=<path>` or the GODOTJS_NODE_CLANG_CL environment variable, and handed to
the code that already knows what to do with clang-cl: it replaces cl_<arch> and adds
-m64/-m32, which is how Chromium's clang-cl Windows build reaches the same place.
The path is used as given, so hand it an absolute one (it usually contains spaces,
which is why an environment variable is the friendlier of the two inputs).

The second hunk is about the architecture name. common.gypi writes
`msvs_configuration_platform` as lowercase "arm64", while this map only listed the
MSBuild spelling "ARM64", so GetArch fell through to its x86 default and an arm64
build silently compiled with the 32-bit toolchain environment.

Verified on Linux by generating the ninja files with the Windows flavour
(./configure --ninja --dest-os=win ..., with GYP_MSVS_OVERRIDE_PATH pointing at a
dummy Visual Studio and -G ninja_use_custom_environment_files=1):

    cl_arm64 = "C:\Program Files\LLVM\bin\clang-cl" -m64
    cl_x64   = "C:\Program Files\LLVM\bin\clang-cl" -m64
    cl_x86   = "C:\Program Files\LLVM\bin\clang-cl" -m32

  without this patch those three name .../MSVC/<version>/bin/Hostx64/x64/cl.exe, and
  the C++ standard Node asks for never reaches the command line.

--- a/tools/gyp/pylib/gyp/generator/ninja.py
+++ b/tools/gyp/pylib/gyp/generator/ninja.py
@@ -2302,6 +2302,19 @@
     if mac_toolchain_dir := generator_flags.get("mac_toolchain_dir", None):
         wrappers["LINK"] = "export DEVELOPER_DIR='%s' &&" % mac_toolchain_dir
 
+    # GodotJS: let the caller name clang-cl without a make_global_settings entry.
+    # The block below already knows how to compile with clang-cl when CC came from
+    # make_global_settings, but gyp requires that entry to be identical in every
+    # .gyp file of the build (assert further down), and Node's build files do not
+    # share one top-level include, so a build file cannot carry it alone. Node 24 and
+    # newer require clang-cl on Windows, so the path is taken from -G clang_cl=<path>
+    # or GODOTJS_NODE_CLANG_CL here, as an absolute path, and used exactly like the
+    # make_global_settings one.
+    if not clang_cl:
+        clang_cl = generator_flags.get(
+            "clang_cl", None
+        ) or os.environ.get("GODOTJS_NODE_CLANG_CL", "")
+
     if flavor == "win":
         configs = [
             target_dicts[qualified_target]["configurations"][config_name]
--- a/tools/gyp/pylib/gyp/msvs_emulation.py
+++ b/tools/gyp/pylib/gyp/msvs_emulation.py
@@ -327,7 +327,13 @@
         if not platform:  # If no specific override, use the configuration's.
             platform = configuration_platform
         # Map from platform to architecture.
-        return {"Win32": "x86", "x64": "x64", "ARM64": "arm64"}.get(platform, "x86")
+        return {
+            "Win32": "x86",
+            "x86": "x86",
+            "x64": "x64",
+            "ARM64": "arm64",
+            "arm64": "arm64",
+        }.get(platform, "x86")
 
     def _TargetConfig(self, config):
         """Returns the target-specific configuration."""
